+- **Shutdown and Recovery.** The last stage happens in macOS Recovery: you shut down, hold the power button until "Loading startup options" appears, choose Omarchy, click Continue, sign in with your macOS account and click **Install** in Omarchy's **Finish setup** (not Startup Security Utility). Plan for it before you start.
 
 ## Install
 
@@ -77,7 +77,7 @@ To install from the disk image instead:
    Each release's notes also give the digest; for 2.3.15 it is `a6ee0cc214fff9f9d194772a5b4492c055c8f74d8572c1183874449d445f3d11`.
 3. Open the disk image and move **Omarchy Installer** to `/Applications`. The disk image holds the app and an `/Applications` link.
 4. Open the app. It checks the Mac model, macOS version, power, FileVault and free disk space, then shows the plan. Choose the disk size, tick the backup box, click **Install**, and enter your macOS administrator account and password when asked. Allow the app to add its helper if macOS asks. The helper shows up under Login Items in System Settings.
-5. Keep the Mac awake and plugged in until the app says the first stage is done. Then follow the on-screen **Finish setup** steps in Recovery (see [What the installer does](#what-the-installer-does)).
+5. Keep the Mac awake and plugged in until the app says the first stage is done. Then shut down, hold the power button until startup options appear, pick the highlighted Omarchy, sign in, then click **Install** in Omarchy's **Finish setup**. Don't use Startup Security Utility: it does not set up Omarchy, and until **Install** has run the Mac can't start Omarchy (see [What the installer does](#what-the-installer-does)).
 
 The release pipeline also copies the dmg and its `.sha256` to `https://mac.omarchy.org/v1/installer/<version>/`; the catalog's `downloadURL` points there ([`docs/catalog-release.md`](docs/catalog-release.md)).
 
@@ -105,7 +105,7 @@ The screens, in order, as the app words them (`PlainLanguage.swift`):
 2. **Plan.** Fetches and verifies the channel's signed catalog (Ed25519, trust root in `Release/trust-root.ed25519.pub`) and downloads the pinned engine and image, checking their sizes and SHA-256 digests before use. Shows the disk bar with macOS, free space and Omarchy, lets you change the Omarchy size, and holds **Install** until the download is verified and the backup box is ticked.
 3. **Authorize.** Takes your macOS administrator account and password, installs the privileged helper and checks that the account is a macOS administrator. The password is not stored in the journal.
 4. **Install.** The helper runs the pinned Asahi-based engine, which goes through these recorded phases: checking the disk; removing a previous Omarchy installation if one exists; preparing disk space (shrinking the macOS APFS container); writing boot files (stub and EFI); preparing the final setup; authorizing startup; installing Omarchy. The app tails the engine journal for progress and verifies the result against the installation record when it finishes. Afterwards the helper writes the encryption choice to `omarchy/install.conf` on the EFI partition.
-5. **Finish setup (Recovery).** The app asks you to shut down, hold the power button until "Loading startup options" appears, choose Omarchy, click Continue and sign in with your macOS account. Omarchy's native Recovery app then asks for the owner password, installs and enrolls startup software, and shows **Installation complete!** with **Restart**. It can also create an optional recovery account (hold Shift on the finished screen, or Utilities → Create Recovery Account…); skipping it can be done later with **Installation → Set Up Recovery Password…** in the main app. See [`Recovery/README.md`](Recovery/README.md).
+5. **Finish setup (Recovery).** The app asks you to shut down, hold the power button until "Loading startup options" appears, pick the highlighted Omarchy, click Continue and sign in with your macOS account, then click **Install** in Omarchy's **Finish setup**, and not to use Startup Security Utility instead. Omarchy's native Recovery app opens by itself, says that this step lets the Mac start Omarchy, asks for the owner password, installs and enrolls startup software, and shows **Installation complete!** with **Restart**. It can also create an optional recovery account (hold Shift on the finished screen, or Utilities → Create Recovery Account…); skipping it can be done later with **Installation → Set Up Recovery Password…** in the main app. See [`Recovery/README.md`](Recovery/README.md).
 6. **First boot of Omarchy.** Choose the Omarchy volume at startup (hold the power button when the Mac starts to pick macOS or Omarchy) and set the disk password. This repository does not cover what happens after that; see the [Mac packages](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy-mac-pkgs).
 
 The helper is a root launchd daemon registered through `SMJobBless`; the app and helper check each other's code signature, and the helper authenticates every XPC client. The destructive steps are guarded by plans bound to a digest of the disk layout, so a plan made for one layout will not run on another.
diff --git a/Recovery/README.md b/Recovery/README.md
index 0f7e2f9..e1c9949 100644
--- a/Recovery/README.md
+++ b/Recovery/README.md
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ The legacy `Finish Installation.app/Contents/Resources/boot.bin` remains for ups
 
 Enrollment rechecks root, the configured target volume and paired power-button 1TR, then resolves a real target volume owner from APFS and target Preboot metadata. When no eligible target owner exists, the explicitly configured `source-volume-owner` bootstrap can use the original macOS owner. That path binds the original System volume and APFS group UUIDs, requires the volume still exists, and intersects copied target administrator metadata with both the specifically authorized source owner UUID recorded by Stage 1 and its current APFS VolumeOwner list. Ambiguous or unmatched owners are rejected. The recorded recovery account takes precedence once APFS reports it as a volume owner; a finalized account that loses ownership is rejected. Existing bputil/kmutil prompt handling stays in the native PTY backend. Normal macOS is rejected before writes.
 
-The first screen, **Finish setup**, is the authorization itself: “Enter password for <owner>. Use your existing macOS owner’s password to set up Omarchy.”, a password field focused at launch, and **Install** (Return). A target owner such as the recovery account gets only the first sentence. The field and button stay disabled while the Recovery environment is checked, at launch and again on Install, as the former Continue button did. A refused password is asked for again in place (“…Enter it again, or choose Utilities → Show Details.”). When a wrong-Recovery issue offers **Set Startup Disk and Shut Down** and selecting the disk needs a password, the same screen becomes **Authorize startup disk** with **Shut Down** and **Cancel**. Other failures still appear as a sheet with **OK**. Repair startup keeps its Restore screen, which needs no password.
+The first screen, **Finish setup**, is the authorization itself. It opens with “This step lets this Mac start Omarchy. Click Install here to finish it. Don’t use Startup Security Utility instead.” (a stub that skips it stays unenrolled), then “Enter password for <owner>. Use your existing macOS owner’s password to set up Omarchy.”, a password field focused at launch, and **Install** (Return). A target owner such as the recovery account gets only the first sentence of the password request. The wrong-Recovery and not-in-Recovery messages also end at **Install** in Finish setup and say not to use Startup Security Utility. The field and button stay disabled while the Recovery environment is checked, at launch and again on Install, as the former Continue button did. A refused password is asked for again in place (“…Enter it again, or choose Utilities → Show Details.”). When a wrong-Recovery issue offers **Set Startup Disk and Shut Down** and selecting the disk needs a password, the same screen becomes **Authorize startup disk** with **Shut Down** and **Cancel**. Other failures still appear as a sheet with **OK**. Repair startup keeps its Restore screen, which needs no password.
 
 The app retains persistent attempt logs and bounded UI log rendering. The log opens from the menu bar's **Utilities → Show Details**, not from a button on the screens; a failure sheet keeps only **OK**, and choosing Show Details while it is open replaces it with the log. Show Details is disabled while any other sheet is open and on the first screen before anything has been logged.
 
